{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q007_chey_like_function_divergence","answer":"SCOPe 2.08 supports probable common ancestry of these domains at the CheY-like superfamily level, c.23.1 (SUNID 52172). They occupy distinct families: CheY-related response regulators, c.23.1.1 (52173); ornithine-decarboxylase N-terminal wing, c.23.1.4 (52252); and KaiA N-terminal domain, c.23.1.5 (82344). All share the Flavodoxin-like fold, c.23, within the alpha/beta class, c. The superfamily supports homology; the fold and class alone would establish only structural similarity.

This relationship does not establish conserved signaling or catalytic activity. CheY-like placement does not make the ornithine-decarboxylase wing or KaiA domain a phosphorylation-dependent response regulator, establish a functional phosphoacceptor aspartate or metal-binding site, or transfer CheY's chemotaxis role and interaction partners. SCOPe explicitly annotates the KaiA family as lacking canonical receiver-domain features. Conversely, the wing's occurrence in ornithine decarboxylase does not establish that the isolated wing catalyzes decarboxylation, nor transfer ornithine specificity or PLP-dependent catalysis to CheY or KaiA. KaiA's circadian-clock role likewise cannot be transferred to the other families, or its whole-protein activity assigned to its N-terminal domain from this classification.

Stronger functional assignments require sequence and active-site comparisons, structural and ligand-bound evidence, full domain architecture and interaction context, and biochemical or signaling experiments supported by primary literature. These were not inspected here. A shared superfamily can guide comparison of the structural core but does not make these domains interchangeable functional templates. For example, the retrieved KaiA domain d1r8ja2 covers residues 2–135, and its annotation identifies a structured linker at 136–176 in a swapped dimer; that context matters for template use.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/api-usage.md","scop-homology/references/biological-interpretation.md","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=CheY&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=ornithine&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=KaiA&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.23.1.1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52253","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52253/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1r8ja2","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1r8ja2/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1u0sy_","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1u0sy_/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52171/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52171/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52172/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52172/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52173/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52173/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52252/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52252/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/82344/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/82344/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52253/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52253/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/107566/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/107566/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/104848/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/104848/homology"]}]}
